WEBVTT

NOTE
A single lecturer at a lectern, professionally captioned. No sound effects,
no music, one speaker, so there are no non-speech annotations and no speaker
labels. Shouted lines are set in capitals on purpose. Fixture, not a
transcript of any real lecture.

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Good morning, everyone.
Thank you for coming.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.700
Today we are going to talk about a
thing most of you never think about.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.900
Captions.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.800
Not the captions on a photograph, but
the ones along the bottom of a video.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.400
Who here has turned
them on this week?

6
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.300
Right. More than half of you.

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.200
That number would have been close
to zero when I started teaching.

8
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.000
Not because hearing has changed.
Because watching has changed.

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.100
People watch on trains. People
watch in bed next to someone asleep.

10
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.300
And in a second language, where the
text carries what the ear misses.

11
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.800
So here is my first claim.

12
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.700
A caption is not a transcript.
It is an edit.

13
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.800
Every line is a decision: what to
keep, what to cut, where to break.

14
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.900
Let me show you what I mean.

15
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.400
Imagine a machine writes down every
word I say, including this one.

16
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.800
Um.

17
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.200
The machine does not know that
"um" is not a word you needed.

18
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.900
The machine does not know
where my sentence ends.

19
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.400
It does not know I just raised
my voice, and that this matters.

20
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.400
GET OUT OF MY LECTURE HALL!

21
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.600
Nobody moved. Good.
That was the demonstration.

22
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.100
A captioner sets that in capitals,
because the volume was the point.

23
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.600
A machine would set it exactly
like the line before it.

24
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.900
Now, I am not here to tell
you machines are useless.

25
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.200
They are a first draft, and a first
draft is a fine place to start.

26
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.700
What I am here to say is that a first
draft is not something you publish.

27
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.200
So let us look at what
correction actually involves.

28
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.700
There are four things a person
fixes that a machine gets wrong.

29
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.900
Punctuation. Case.

30
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.800
Line breaks. And timing.

31
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.700
We will take them one at a time,
and I promise to stop shouting.

32
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.900
Mostly.

NOTE
The lecture continues after a break. The second half is here so the file
runs past the detector's duration threshold for non-speech annotations,
which is the signal a single-speaker file must survive.

33
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.400
Welcome back. Let us pick
up with punctuation.

34
00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:07.900
A full stop says the thought is over.
A question mark says wait.

35
00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:11.500
Machines put neither
where a person would.

36
00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.000
Case is next, and it
is the easiest to see.

37
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.500
A sentence starts with a capital.
A name has one. A shout has them all.

38
00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.100
A machine gives you none
of that, or all of it.

39
00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:28.900
Line breaks are where
the craft lives.

40
00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:33.400
You break where the phrase breaks,
never in the middle of a name.

41
00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:37.300
And timing is where
the audience lives.

42
00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:41.700
A late caption is a joke explained
after everyone has stopped laughing.

43
00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.200
Thank you. Questions?
